\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qc\f0\fs24\lang9\par
\b\fs36 Automated Test Log Analysis Using Machine Learning\fs32\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\fs24\par
\fs32 Abstract :\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl360\slmult1\qj\b0\fs28 With the increasing complexity of modern software systems, automated testing generates massive volumes of execution logs that are difficult to analyze manually. Efficient identification of test outcomes, failure patterns, and root causes has therefore become a critical challenge. This project presents an intelligent, end-to-end framework for automated test log analysis using machine learning and natural language processing techniques. The system ingests raw test logs, transforms them into structured representations, and applies preprocessing and feature engineering to extract meaningful patterns. Failed and aborted test cases are analyzed independently through clustering techniques to uncover recurring behavioral trends, and the resulting clusters are combined to enable consistent root cause tagging. Supervised machine learning models are then trained to classify test execution status and to predict root causes for both failure and abort scenarios. By automating the complete analysis pipeline from raw logs to actionable insights, the proposed solution significantly reduces manual debugging effort, improves diagnostic accuracy, and enhances the overall efficiency and reliability of the software testing process.\par
\line\b\fs32 Introduction :\b0\fs28\line In modern software development, automated testing is widely used to ensure the quality, reliability, and correctness of complex software systems. As testing scales across multiple configurations, devices, and continuous integration environments, a large volume of test execution logs is generated for every test cycle. These logs contain critical information related to test behavior, execution status, failures, and system conditions. However, analyzing such logs manually is a time-consuming and error-prone process that requires significant domain expertise.\par
Traditional approaches to log analysis primarily rely on manual inspection or rule-based scripts, which are limited in scalability and adaptability. As test environments evolve, new failure patterns emerge, making it difficult to maintain static rules and consistently identify root causes. This often results in delayed debugging, increased test cycle time, and reduced overall productivity.\par
To overcome these challenges, this project proposes an automated test log analysis framework that leverages machine learning and natural language processing techniques. The system processes raw test logs, extracts relevant information, and transforms unstructured log data into meaningful features. Using a combination of clustering techniques and supervised learning models, the framework classifies test execution status, identifies recurring failure and abort patterns, and predicts root causes with associated recommendations.\par
The proposed solution aims to minimize manual debugging effort by providing structured insights into test results. By automating failure detection, pattern identification, and root cause analysis, the system improves the efficiency, accuracy, and consistency of the software testing process. This project demonstrates how intelligent log analysis can significantly enhance test diagnostics and support faster decision-making in modern testing environments.\par
\par
\b\fs32 Problem Statement :\b0\fs28\line Manual analysis of test execution logs is time-consuming and requires significant human effort. With large volumes of unstructured and verbose log data, identifying test outcomes and recurring failure patterns becomes difficult and error-prone. Detecting root causes often depends heavily on domain expertise and manual debugging, which delays issue resolution and increases test cycle time. Moreover, there is no automated mechanism to provide actionable recommendations for failures or aborted test cases, making the process inefficient and prone to inconsistencies.\par
\par
