\b\fs32 Data Description\b0\fs28\par
The data used in this project consists of automated test execution log files generated during system and protocol validation. These logs are unstructured text files containing detailed information about test cases, execution steps, timestamps, protocol messages, errors, warnings, and final test results such as PASS, FAIL, or ABORT.\par
The raw log data is processed to extract relevant fields including test case name, device under test (DUT), configuration details, execution timestamps, status, and error messages. Error-related lines such as failures, mismatches, timeouts, and abort reasons are identified and used as primary inputs for analysis. The extracted information is converted into structured CSV format to support preprocessing, feature engineering, and model training.\par
For machine learning tasks, the textual error content is transformed into numerical representations using TF-IDF vectorization. Labeled datasets are created for status classification and root cause prediction, and the data is split into training and testing sets to evaluate model performance. Overall, the dataset represents large-scale, real-world test logs with varying formats and failure patterns, enabling robust training and validation of the automated log analysis system.\par
\b\fs32\par
Feature Engineering\b0\fs28\par
Feature engineering is performed to transform preprocessed log data into structured and meaningful numerical features suitable for machine learning models. The process begins by loading the preprocessed log dataset and assigning a stable row identifier to each record. Test execution status values are normalized and filtered to include only valid outcomes such as PASS, FAIL, and ABORT. Timestamps are parsed and sorted by device under test (DUT), test case, and execution time to enable time-based feature generation.\par
Frequency-based features are generated to capture recurring failure and abort patterns. These include the number of failures and aborts per test suite and per DUT, helping the model identify unstable components and frequently failing environments. Temporal features are derived by calculating the time elapsed since the last failure and the last abort for each test case, providing historical context about repeated issues. Synthetic execution duration values are also added to simulate test runtime behavior.\par
Recent execution behavior is represented using status indicator flags, including binary flags for recent failures and aborts, as well as a consolidated status flag encoding PASS, FAIL, and ABORT states. Configuration-related information is encoded by hashing configuration values into numerical representations, ensuring compatibility with machine learning models while preserving uniqueness. Missing or unavailable environment details are handled gracefully using default values.\par
Error message fields are standardized to ensure consistency, with non-error executions explicitly marked to avoid noise during training. Finally, the engineered features are stored in a structured CSV file, forming the final dataset used for clustering, model training, and inference. This comprehensive feature set enables accurate prediction of test status and root cause classification.\f0\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\par
\b\fs32 Machine Learning Models\b0\fs28\par
This project utilizes both unsupervised and supervised machine learning models to automate test log analysis and root cause identification.\par
\b Failure and Abort Clustering Models\par
\b0 Unsupervised clustering models are used to group similar failure and abort log messages based on textual similarity. Log error messages are converted into numerical representations using embedding techniques, and similarity-based clustering is applied to identify recurring patterns. These clusters help in discovering common failure and abort categories and form the basis for root cause labeling.\par
\b Status Classification Model\par
\b0 A supervised Random Forest classifier is used to predict the execution status of test cases as PASS, FAIL, or ABORT. The model is trained using engineered numerical and contextual features derived from log data. Random Forest is chosen for its ensemble learning approach, robustness to noise, and strong performance on complex datasets.\par
\b Root Cause Prediction Models\par
\b0 Separate supervised Random Forest models are trained for predicting root causes of failed and aborted test cases. These models use TF-IDF\f1\endash based text features extracted from error messages along with contextual features. By maintaining independent models for FAIL and ABORT cases, the system achieves more accurate and interpretable root cause predictions.\par
\par
\b\fs32 ABORT / FAIL Case Handling\par
\b0\fs28 The system handles FAIL and ABORT test cases separately to ensure accurate analysis and meaningful root cause identification. When a test execution is marked as FAIL, the system extracts relevant error messages from the log file and applies a trained failure root cause prediction model. These failures typically indicate functional or protocol-related issues, and the predicted root cause is mapped to a predefined recommendation to guide corrective action.\par
In the case of ABORTed test executions, the system applies a different handling strategy. Abort scenarios usually occur due to environmental issues, unmet preconditions, unsupported configurations, or user intervention. If an abort is caused by explicit user action, such as a manual stop, the system skips root cause prediction and no recommendation is generated. For all other abort cases, error messages are analyzed using a dedicated abort root cause prediction model. The predicted root cause is then mapped to an appropriate recommendation.\par
By separating FAIL and ABORT handling, the system improves prediction accuracy and ensures that recommendations are context-aware and actionable. This approach avoids misclassification and allows the system to respond appropriately to different types of test execution failures.\par
\b\fs32\par
Execution Steps\par
\fs28 Log Input\b0\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj The process begins with collecting raw log files generated by the Device Under Test (DUT) during automated testing.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}These logs contain detailed execution information including timestamps, test case IDs, configurations, protocol messages, errors, warnings, and results such as PASS, FAIL, or ABORT.\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b Preprocessing\b0\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj Raw logs are often unstructured and contain noise. Preprocessing involves cleaning the logs, removing irrelevant lines, normalizing status values, and handling missing or inconsistent entries.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Relevant information such as test case identifiers, DUT details, timestamps, and error messages are extracted and structured for further analysis.\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b Feature Engineering\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0 Structured features are created to capture important aspects of test executions:\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Frequency-based features: Number of failures or aborts per suite and DUT.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Temporal features: Time since the last failure or abort for each test case.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Execution duration: Synthetic runtime of test cases.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Status flags: Indicators for recent failures, aborts, or PASS.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Configuration encoding: Converts DUT configuration details into numerical representations.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Error messages: Standardized text features for use in ML models.\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b Clustering (Failure & Abort Logs)\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0 Unsupervised clustering groups similar log messages for failures and aborts separately.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}This step identifies recurring patterns and common issues, which helps in understanding the behavior of the system under test.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Clusters are then merged for consistent root cause labeling.\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b Root Cause Tagging\b0\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj Each cluster of log messages is assigned a meaningful root cause label, either for a FAIL or ABORT case.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}This provides labeled data that serves as input for supervised learning models.\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b Train\endash Test Split\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0 The labeled dataset is split into training and testing sets to allow proper evaluation of machine learning models.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}This ensures the models are validated on unseen data for accurate performance measurement.\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b Model Training\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b0 Status Classification Model: Random Forest classifier trained to predict PASS, FAIL, or ABORT.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}FAIL Root Cause Model: Random Forest classifier trained to predict specific root causes for failed test cases.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}ABORT Root Cause Model: Random Forest classifier trained for predicting root causes of aborted test cases.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Random Forest is chosen for its robustness, ability to handle high-dimensional features, and resistance to overfitting.\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b Preprocessing Freeze\b0\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj All preprocessing steps used during training (such as TF-IDF vectorization, feature scaling, and encoding) are saved.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}This ensures consistency during inference and prevents discrepancies between training and testing data.\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\b Inference and Recommendation Generation\b0\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\ri-924\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj New log files are processed using the frozen preprocessing steps.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Status is predicted using the trained status model, and root cause is predicted using the corresponding root cause model (FAIL or ABORT).\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Rule-based mappings are applied to generate actionable recommendations based on the predicted root cause.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Special handling is applied for user-initiated ABORTs, where recommendations are not needed.\par

\pard\ri-924\sa200\sl360\slmult1\qj\b Results\b0\par
The automated log analysis system produces a structured and user-friendly output for each analyzed test execution log. The results include the test status, the predicted root cause (for FAIL or ABORT cases), and actionable recommendations based on predefined mappings.\par
